    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Equation"/>
   </text:sequence-decls>
   <text:p text:style-name="P15">Enhancing the German Transmission Grid through Dynamic Line Rating</text:p>
   <text:section text:style-name="Sect1" text:name="TextSection">
    <text:p text:style-name="P2">Philipp Glaum</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P9">Fabian Hofmann</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P1">Institute of Energy Technology </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P1">Technical University Berlin</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P1">Berlin, Germany</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P1">p.glaum@tu-berlin.de</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P2"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P2"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P2"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P1"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P3"/>
   </text:section>
   <text:section text:style-name="Sect2" text:name="Section1">
    <text:p text:style-name="Abstract"><text:span text:style-name="T24">Abstract</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">—According to recently announced plans of the German Federal Government, the German power system is facing an ongoing transition from conventional to renewable power generation, which should supply 80% of the total demand by 2030. One key task lays in reorganizing the transmission system such that power can be transported from sites with good renewable potentials to the load centers. As the literature shows a system-wide implementation of Dynamic Line Rating (DLR), a dynamic calculation of the transmission line capacities based on prevailing weather conditions, <text:s/>can act as an important complement to building new transmission lines. On behalf of a detailed </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">power system model </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">including all of today’s extra high voltage transmission lines and substations, we show how synergies between DLR and an increased wind power generation leverage, leading to an overall cost-benefit of XX% for the 2030 goals compared to a system without DLR. </text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P8">Index Terms—Power system analysis computing, Power System Management, Power System Planning, Renewable energy sources</text:p>
    <text:list xml:id="list882331971" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P17">Introduction </text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T1">The deployment of new renewable energy sources far from load centers is leading to an increasing need for grid capacity. A notable example can be found in Germany, where most of the wind energy is generated in the North while there is significant industrial demand in the South </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;jiWJxcBB&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[1]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[1]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:976,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/K79FW26P&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:976,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Landscape Research&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1080/01426397.2017.1300639&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0142-6397, 1469-9710&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;4&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Landscape Research&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: kuhneConflictsNegotiationProcesses2018&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;529–541&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Conflicts and negotiation processes in the course of power grid extension in Germany&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01426397.2017.1300639&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;43&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kühne&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Olaf&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Weber&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Florian&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,21]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,5,19]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;kuhneConflictsNegotiationProcesses2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDdRmMCfLtXb"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[1]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;jiWJxcBB&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[1]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[1]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:976,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/K79FW26P&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:976,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Landscape Research&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1080/01426397.2017.1300639&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0142-6397, 1469-9710&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;4&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Landscape Research&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: kuhneConflictsNegotiationProcesses2018&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;529–541&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Conflicts and negotiation processes in the course of power grid extension in Germany&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01426397.2017.1300639&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;43&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kühne&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Olaf&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Weber&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Florian&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,21]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,5,19]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;kuhneConflictsNegotiationProcesses2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDdRmMCfLtXb"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. Whereas the current federal government plans to add </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T2">~</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">70 GW wind power capacity until 2030, only a few grid expansion projects have been realized </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;5o6yC2BJ&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[2], [3]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[2], [3]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:980,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/U2JA3CPY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:980,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;german&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: federalministryofeconomicaffairsandclimateactionGermanyCurrentClimate2022&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;37&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Germany&apos;s current climate action status&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.bmwi.de/Redaktion/EN/Downloads/E/germany-s-current-climate-action-status.html&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;literal&quot;:&quot;Federal Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate Action&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,28]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,13]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;federalministryofeconomicaffairsandclimateactionGermanyCurrentClimate2022&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:928,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/F82JDDBJ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:928,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;webpage&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Die Energy-Charts bieten interaktive Grafiken zu: Stromproduktion, Stromerzeugung, Emissionen, Klimadaten, Spotmarktpreisen, Szenarien zur Energiewende und eine umfangreiche Kartenanwendung zu: Kraftwerken, Übertragungsleitungen und Meteodaten., Deutschland&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Energy-Charts&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://energy-charts.info/?l=de&amp;c=DE&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Burger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Prof Dr Bruno&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,28]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;burgerEnergyCharts&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDKmUtgtyAra"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[2], [3]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;5o6yC2BJ&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[2], [3]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[2], [3]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:980,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/U2JA3CPY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:980,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;german&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: federalministryofeconomicaffairsandclimateactionGermanyCurrentClimate2022&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;37&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Germany&apos;s current climate action status&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.bmwi.de/Redaktion/EN/Downloads/E/germany-s-current-climate-action-status.html&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;literal&quot;:&quot;Federal Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate Action&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,28]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,13]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;federalministryofeconomicaffairsandclimateactionGermanyCurrentClimate2022&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:928,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/F82JDDBJ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:928,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;webpage&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Die Energy-Charts bieten interaktive Grafiken zu: Stromproduktion, Stromerzeugung, Emissionen, Klimadaten, Spotmarktpreisen, Szenarien zur Energiewende und eine umfangreiche Kartenanwendung zu: Kraftwerken, Übertragungsleitungen und Meteodaten., Deutschland&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Energy-Charts&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://energy-charts.info/?l=de&amp;c=DE&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Burger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Prof Dr Bruno&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,28]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;burgerEnergyCharts&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDKmUtgtyAra"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. Moreover, most of these projects have been delayed due to administrative problems and protest activities </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;beKpvg8v&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[4], [5]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[4], [5]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:975,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/8XGUMP97&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:975,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Background: Among the major players in German energy politics, a broad consensus exists that one important condition for the transition to renewable energies is to expand the electricity transmission grid. More than 7000 km of new or extended power routes are planned, generally as overhead lines. Mainly because of local protests, many projects, however, lag behind their original timetable. The protest initiatives are often supported by concerned municipalities and districts, environmental organizations, and scientists. This paper examines the most conflicted projects and inquires whether the protests are part of a social movement. Methods: The study aims for a better understanding of these protests. It therefore explores the opponents’ action orientations and the relevant action fields. The main method is a comparative qualitative case study that is based on a document analysis. The latter was supported by quantitative considerations. Results: Whereas homogeneity in local and regional contexts predominates, across the regions, the protests often strongly diverge in respect to their dominant frames and action orientations. This occurs in a diverse structure of action fields. If the protests are strong and fundamentally oriented, the conflicts take place mainly in state field and discursive arenas. Conclusions: Although the grid extension protests share characteristics with the activities against war, discrimination, and nuclear power use, they are not a social movement in the narrow sense. Rather, they are a collection of single-point protests that occasionally transcend their own boundaries to take on a more coherent and unified character.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy, Sustainability and Society&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1186/s13705-016-0069-9&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2192-0567&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;1&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energ Sustain Soc&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: neukirchProtestsGermanElectricity2016&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;4&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Protests against German electricity grid extension as a new social movement? A journey into the areas of conflict&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;Protests against German electricity grid extension as a new social movement?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://energsustainsoc.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13705-016-0069-9&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neukirch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Mario&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,21]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;neukirchProtestsGermanElectricity2016&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:981,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SSXCJGDY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:981,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.energy.2016.08.022&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;03605442&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energy&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;465-477&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Public acceptance and preferences related to renewable energy and grid expansion policy: Empirical insights for Germany&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;Public acceptance and preferences related to renewable energy and grid expansion policy&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0360544216311252&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;114&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Bertsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Valentin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hall&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Margeret&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Weinhardt&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Christof&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Fichtner&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Wolf&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,11]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;bertschPublicAcceptancePreferences2016&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND9WimzudutY"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[4], [5]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;beKpvg8v&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[4], [5]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[4], [5]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:975,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/8XGUMP97&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:975,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Background: Among the major players in German energy politics, a broad consensus exists that one important condition for the transition to renewable energies is to expand the electricity transmission grid. More than 7000 km of new or extended power routes are planned, generally as overhead lines. Mainly because of local protests, many projects, however, lag behind their original timetable. The protest initiatives are often supported by concerned municipalities and districts, environmental organizations, and scientists. This paper examines the most conflicted projects and inquires whether the protests are part of a social movement. Methods: The study aims for a better understanding of these protests. It therefore explores the opponents’ action orientations and the relevant action fields. The main method is a comparative qualitative case study that is based on a document analysis. The latter was supported by quantitative considerations. Results: Whereas homogeneity in local and regional contexts predominates, across the regions, the protests often strongly diverge in respect to their dominant frames and action orientations. This occurs in a diverse structure of action fields. If the protests are strong and fundamentally oriented, the conflicts take place mainly in state field and discursive arenas. Conclusions: Although the grid extension protests share characteristics with the activities against war, discrimination, and nuclear power use, they are not a social movement in the narrow sense. Rather, they are a collection of single-point protests that occasionally transcend their own boundaries to take on a more coherent and unified character.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy, Sustainability and Society&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1186/s13705-016-0069-9&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2192-0567&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;1&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energ Sustain Soc&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: neukirchProtestsGermanElectricity2016&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;4&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Protests against German electricity grid extension as a new social movement? A journey into the areas of conflict&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;Protests against German electricity grid extension as a new social movement?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://energsustainsoc.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13705-016-0069-9&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neukirch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Mario&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,21]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;neukirchProtestsGermanElectricity2016&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:981,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SSXCJGDY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:981,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.energy.2016.08.022&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;03605442&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energy&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;465-477&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Public acceptance and preferences related to renewable energy and grid expansion policy: Empirical insights for Germany&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;Public acceptance and preferences related to renewable energy and grid expansion policy&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0360544216311252&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;114&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Bertsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Valentin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hall&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Margeret&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Weinhardt&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Christof&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Fichtner&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Wolf&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,11]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;bertschPublicAcceptancePreferences2016&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND9WimzudutY"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. This trend will likely exacerbate transmission system congestion, which may lead to more grid instability and curtailment </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;Zt6sunze&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[6]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[6]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:983,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/FNHQMAWY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:983,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Electrical energy storage (EES) is a promising flexibility source for prospective low-carbon energy systems. In the last couple of years, many studies for EES capacity planning have been produced. However, these resulted in a very broad range of power and energy capacity requirements for storage, making it difficult for policymakers to identify clear storage planning recommendations. Therefore, we studied 17 recent storage expansion studies pertinent to the U.S., Europe, and Germany. We then systemized the storage requirement per variable renewable energy (VRE) share and generation technology. Our synthesis reveals that with increasing VRE shares, the EES power capacity increases linearly; and the energy capacity, exponentially. Further, by analyzing the outliers, the EES energy requirements can be at least halved. It becomes clear that grids dominated by photovoltaic energy call for more EES, while large shares of wind rely more on transmission capacity. Taking into account the energy mix clarifies—to a large degree—the apparent conflict of the storage requirements between the existing studies. Finally, there might exist a negative bias towards storage because transmission costs are frequently optimistic (by neglecting execution delays and social opposition) and storage can cope with uncertainties, but these issues are rarely acknowledged in the planning process.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Cleaner Production&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.01.144&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0959-6526&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Journal of Cleaner Production&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;449-459&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;How much electrical energy storage do we need? A synthesis for the U.S., Europe, and Germany&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;How much electrical energy storage do we need?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959652618301665&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;181&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cebulla&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Felix&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Haas&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jannik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Eichman&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Josh&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Nowak&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Wolfgang&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mancarella&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Pierluigi&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;cebullaHowMuchElectrical2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDBrzUNihJlX"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[6]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;Zt6sunze&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[6]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[6]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:983,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/FNHQMAWY&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:983,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Electrical energy storage (EES) is a promising flexibility source for prospective low-carbon energy systems. In the last couple of years, many studies for EES capacity planning have been produced. However, these resulted in a very broad range of power and energy capacity requirements for storage, making it difficult for policymakers to identify clear storage planning recommendations. Therefore, we studied 17 recent storage expansion studies pertinent to the U.S., Europe, and Germany. We then systemized the storage requirement per variable renewable energy (VRE) share and generation technology. Our synthesis reveals that with increasing VRE shares, the EES power capacity increases linearly; and the energy capacity, exponentially. Further, by analyzing the outliers, the EES energy requirements can be at least halved. It becomes clear that grids dominated by photovoltaic energy call for more EES, while large shares of wind rely more on transmission capacity. Taking into account the energy mix clarifies—to a large degree—the apparent conflict of the storage requirements between the existing studies. Finally, there might exist a negative bias towards storage because transmission costs are frequently optimistic (by neglecting execution delays and social opposition) and storage can cope with uncertainties, but these issues are rarely acknowledged in the planning process.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Cleaner Production&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.01.144&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0959-6526&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Journal of Cleaner Production&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;449-459&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;How much electrical energy storage do we need? A synthesis for the U.S., Europe, and Germany&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;How much electrical energy storage do we need?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959652618301665&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;181&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cebulla&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Felix&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Haas&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jannik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Eichman&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Josh&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Nowak&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Wolfgang&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mancarella&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Pierluigi&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;cebullaHowMuchElectrical2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDBrzUNihJlX"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. There is therefore a strong incentive to exploit existing grid infrastructure more fully.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T6">To overcome the mismatch between slow installation of new transmission lines and rapid installation of new renewable generation, several </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">approaches</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6"> can be taken that must be reconciled: </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">(1) l</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">arge scale implementation of </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">storage facilities </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T35">in order </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">to flatten </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T36">power </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">feed-in</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T36">s</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T36">and power demands </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;ozdZ3fuq&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[7]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[7]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:989,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/S8PJM88K&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:989,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Future energy supply systems must become more flexible than they are today to accommodate the significant contributions expected from intermittent renewable power sources. Although numerous studies on planning flexibility options have emerged over the last few years, the uncertainties related to model-based studies have left the literature lacking a proper understanding of the investment strategy needed to ensure robust power grid expansion. To address this issue, we focus herein on two important aspects of these uncertainties: the first is the relevance of various social preferences for the use of certain technologies, and the second is how the available approaches affect the flexibility options for power transmission in energy system models. To address these uncertainties, we analyze a host of scenarios. We use an energy system optimization model to plan the transition of Europe’s energy system. In addition to interacting with the heating and transport sectors, the model integrates power flows in three different ways: as a transport model, as a direct current power flow model, and as a linearized alternating current power flow model based on profiles of power transfer distribution factors. The results show that deploying transmission systems contribute significantly to system adequacy. If investments in new power transmission infrastructure are restricted—for example, because of social opposition—additional power generation and storage technologies are an alternative option to reach the necessary level of adequacy at 2% greater system costs. The share of power transmission in total system costs remains widely stable around 1.5%, even if cost assumptions or the approaches for modeling power flows are varied. Thus, the results indicate the importance of promoting investments in infrastructure projects that support pan-European power transmission. However, a wide range of possibilities exists to put this strategy into practice.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Frontiers in Energy Research&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2296-598X&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Frontiers&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;To Prevent or Promote Grid Expansion? Analyzing the Future Role of Power Transmission in the European Energy System&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;To Prevent or Promote Grid Expansion?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fenrg.2020.541495&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;8&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cao&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Karl-Kiên&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Pregger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Thomas&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Haas&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jannik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Lens&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Hendrik&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;caoPreventPromoteGrid2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDxAqJxrFett"/><text:span text:style-name="T36">[7]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;ozdZ3fuq&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[7]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[7]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:989,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/S8PJM88K&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:989,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Future energy supply systems must become more flexible than they are today to accommodate the significant contributions expected from intermittent renewable power sources. Although numerous studies on planning flexibility options have emerged over the last few years, the uncertainties related to model-based studies have left the literature lacking a proper understanding of the investment strategy needed to ensure robust power grid expansion. To address this issue, we focus herein on two important aspects of these uncertainties: the first is the relevance of various social preferences for the use of certain technologies, and the second is how the available approaches affect the flexibility options for power transmission in energy system models. To address these uncertainties, we analyze a host of scenarios. We use an energy system optimization model to plan the transition of Europe’s energy system. In addition to interacting with the heating and transport sectors, the model integrates power flows in three different ways: as a transport model, as a direct current power flow model, and as a linearized alternating current power flow model based on profiles of power transfer distribution factors. The results show that deploying transmission systems contribute significantly to system adequacy. If investments in new power transmission infrastructure are restricted—for example, because of social opposition—additional power generation and storage technologies are an alternative option to reach the necessary level of adequacy at 2% greater system costs. The share of power transmission in total system costs remains widely stable around 1.5%, even if cost assumptions or the approaches for modeling power flows are varied. Thus, the results indicate the importance of promoting investments in infrastructure projects that support pan-European power transmission. However, a wide range of possibilities exists to put this strategy into practice.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Frontiers in Energy Research&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2296-598X&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Frontiers&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;To Prevent or Promote Grid Expansion? Analyzing the Future Role of Power Transmission in the European Energy System&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;To Prevent or Promote Grid Expansion?&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fenrg.2020.541495&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;8&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cao&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Karl-Kiên&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Pregger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Thomas&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Haas&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jannik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Lens&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Hendrik&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;caoPreventPromoteGrid2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDxAqJxrFett"/><text:span text:style-name="T36">; </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T37">(2) u</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">sage of a</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">lternative energy carrier networks </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">to relieve the electricity grid </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;cVPXyDQ2&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[8]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[8]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:987,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/ZEFDU8VZ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:987,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;For this study, a spatially and temporally resolved optimization model was used to investigate and economically evaluate pathways for using surplus electricity to cover positive residual loads by means of different technologies to reconvert hydrogen into electricity. The associated technology pathways consist of electrolyzers, salt caverns, hydrogen pipelines, power cables, and various technologies for reconversion into electricity. The investigations were conducted based on an energy scenario for 2050 in which surplus electricity from northern Germany is available to cover the electricity grid load in the federal state of North Rhine-Westphalia (NRW). A key finding of the pathway analysis is that NRW&apos;s electricity demand can be covered entirely by renewable energy sources in this scenario, which involves CO2 savings of 44.4 million tons of CO2/a in comparison to the positive residual load being covered from a conventional power plant fleet. The pathway involving CCGT (combined cycle gas turbines) as hydrogen reconversion option was identified as being the most cost effective (total investment: € 43.1 billion, electricity generation costs of reconversion: € 176/MWh). Large-scale hydrogen storage and reconversion as well as the use of the hydrogen infrastructure built for this purpose can make a meaningful contribution to the expansion of the electricity grid. However, for reasons of efficiency, substituting the electricity grid expansion entirely with hydrogen reconversion systems does not make sense from an economic standpoint. Furthermore, the hydrogen reconversion pathways evaluated, including large-scale storage, significantly contribute to the security of the energy supply and to secured power generation capacities.&quot;,&quot;collection-title&quot;:&quot;Special Issue on Power To Gas and Hydrogen applications to energy systems at different scales - Building, District and National level&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Hydrogen Energy&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.11.194&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0360-3199&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;19&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Hydrogen Energy&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;9594-9607&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Design and evaluation of hydrogen electricity reconversion pathways in national energy systems using spatially and temporally resolved energy system optimization&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0360319918338552&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;44&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Welder&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Lara&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Stenzel&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Peter&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Ebersbach&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Natalie&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Markewitz&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Peter&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Robinius&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Martin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Emonts&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Bernd&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Stolten&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Detlef&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,4,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;welderDesignEvaluationHydrogen2019&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND1i8iN5IAKi"/><text:span text:style-name="T5">[8]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;cVPXyDQ2&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[8]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[8]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:987,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/ZEFDU8VZ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:987,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;For this study, a spatially and temporally resolved optimization model was used to investigate and economically evaluate pathways for using surplus electricity to cover positive residual loads by means of different technologies to reconvert hydrogen into electricity. The associated technology pathways consist of electrolyzers, salt caverns, hydrogen pipelines, power cables, and various technologies for reconversion into electricity. The investigations were conducted based on an energy scenario for 2050 in which surplus electricity from northern Germany is available to cover the electricity grid load in the federal state of North Rhine-Westphalia (NRW). A key finding of the pathway analysis is that NRW&apos;s electricity demand can be covered entirely by renewable energy sources in this scenario, which involves CO2 savings of 44.4 million tons of CO2/a in comparison to the positive residual load being covered from a conventional power plant fleet. The pathway involving CCGT (combined cycle gas turbines) as hydrogen reconversion option was identified as being the most cost effective (total investment: € 43.1 billion, electricity generation costs of reconversion: € 176/MWh). Large-scale hydrogen storage and reconversion as well as the use of the hydrogen infrastructure built for this purpose can make a meaningful contribution to the expansion of the electricity grid. However, for reasons of efficiency, substituting the electricity grid expansion entirely with hydrogen reconversion systems does not make sense from an economic standpoint. Furthermore, the hydrogen reconversion pathways evaluated, including large-scale storage, significantly contribute to the security of the energy supply and to secured power generation capacities.&quot;,&quot;collection-title&quot;:&quot;Special Issue on Power To Gas and Hydrogen applications to energy systems at different scales - Building, District and National level&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Hydrogen Energy&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.11.194&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0360-3199&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;19&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Hydrogen Energy&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;9594-9607&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Design and evaluation of hydrogen electricity reconversion pathways in national energy systems using spatially and temporally resolved energy system optimization&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0360319918338552&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;44&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Welder&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Lara&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Stenzel&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Peter&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Ebersbach&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Natalie&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Markewitz&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Peter&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Robinius&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Martin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Emonts&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Bernd&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Stolten&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Detlef&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,4,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;welderDesignEvaluationHydrogen2019&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND1i8iN5IAKi"/><text:span text:style-name="T5">; </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">(3) l</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">everaging the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">existing grid infrastructure to increase the operating capacity. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">Note that in a smart setup, these points complement each other. In the following, we will focus on the last point. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">In</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"> comparison to the first two points, it is </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">straight-forward, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">cheap</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">and non-invasiv </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">to implement and does not requires </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">conversion technologies which (yet) suffer from high prices and comparably low round-trip efficiencies </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;aO2dj2JM&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[9], [10]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[9], [10]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:991,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/JPNT4M99&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:991,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The distribution of renewable energy is now a reality in power distribution grids. This type of energy demands a new and effective electrical infrastructure to manage its intermittency. A dynamic line rating system provides an alternative for new assets with a large capital investment. Dynamic line rating studies and projects have revealed several issues that need to be solved to create safe and accurate systems. One of the major challenges is finding the best monitoring point. Because the ampacity changes along the overhead line, the objective of this study was to develop a methodology to calculate the distributed ampacity along the line using digital elevation and wind propagation models. Finally, an example of this methodology was applied to two real distribution lines: a short line and long line. A comparison between the new methodology and the current operation methodologies found that the point with the least cooling along the line differed depending on the wind direction. Thus, the ampacity could be overestimated by the commonly used dynamic line rating systems. Moreover, the proposed Simulated Distributed Wind Estimation (SWDE) is an economical alternative to a distributed monitoring system.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Electrical Power &amp; Energy Systems&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.ijepes.2021.107338&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0142-0615&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Electrical Power &amp; Energy Systems&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;107338&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Application of Digital Elevation Models to wind estimation for dynamic line rating&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0142061521005779&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;134&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Minguez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Martinez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Manana&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;M.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cuasante&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;D.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Garañeda&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,1]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;minguezApplicationDigitalElevation2022&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:994,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/8UQNDLLZ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:994,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Ergebnispapier des dena-Stakeholderprozess „Höhere Auslastung des Bestandsnetzes“&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.dena.de/fileadmin/dena/Dokumente/Pdf/9209_Ergebnispapier_dena-Stakeholder-Prozess-Hoehere_Auslastung_Stromnetze.pdf&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Deutsche Energie-Agentur GmbH (dena)&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;deutscheenergie-agenturgmbhdenaErgebnispapierDenaStakeholderprozessHohere&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDwkyXJuOUCD"/><text:span text:style-name="T10">[9], [10]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;aO2dj2JM&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[9], [10]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[9], [10]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:991,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/JPNT4M99&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:991,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The distribution of renewable energy is now a reality in power distribution grids. This type of energy demands a new and effective electrical infrastructure to manage its intermittency. A dynamic line rating system provides an alternative for new assets with a large capital investment. Dynamic line rating studies and projects have revealed several issues that need to be solved to create safe and accurate systems. One of the major challenges is finding the best monitoring point. Because the ampacity changes along the overhead line, the objective of this study was to develop a methodology to calculate the distributed ampacity along the line using digital elevation and wind propagation models. Finally, an example of this methodology was applied to two real distribution lines: a short line and long line. A comparison between the new methodology and the current operation methodologies found that the point with the least cooling along the line differed depending on the wind direction. Thus, the ampacity could be overestimated by the commonly used dynamic line rating systems. Moreover, the proposed Simulated Distributed Wind Estimation (SWDE) is an economical alternative to a distributed monitoring system.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Electrical Power &amp; Energy Systems&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.ijepes.2021.107338&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0142-0615&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;International Journal of Electrical Power &amp; Energy Systems&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;107338&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;ScienceDirect&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Application of Digital Elevation Models to wind estimation for dynamic line rating&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0142061521005779&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;134&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Minguez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Martinez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Manana&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;M.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Cuasante&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;D.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Garañeda&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;R.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,1]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;minguezApplicationDigitalElevation2022&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:994,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/8UQNDLLZ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:994,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Ergebnispapier des dena-Stakeholderprozess „Höhere Auslastung des Bestandsnetzes“&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.dena.de/fileadmin/dena/Dokumente/Pdf/9209_Ergebnispapier_dena-Stakeholder-Prozess-Hoehere_Auslastung_Stromnetze.pdf&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Deutsche Energie-Agentur GmbH (dena)&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,4,20]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;deutscheenergie-agenturgmbhdenaErgebnispapierDenaStakeholderprozessHohere&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDwkyXJuOUCD"/><text:span text:style-name="T10">. </text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P13"><text:span text:style-name="T1">Traditionally, transmission line capacity is calculated assuming unfavorable, static weather conditions such as 40°C <text:s/>ambient temperature and 0.6m/s wind speed </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;TF67pkp6&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[11]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[11]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:968,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/RLNSVWVG&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:968,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;When a wind power system is connected to a network point there is a limit of power generation based on the characteristics of the network and the loads connected to it. Traditionally, transmission line limits are estimated conservatively assuming unfavourable weather conditions (high ambient temperature, full sun and low wind speed). However, the transmission capacity of an overhead line increases when wind speed is high, due to the cooling caused by wind in the distribution lines.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.rser.2015.07.149&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;13640321&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: fernandezReviewDynamicLine2016&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;80–92&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Review of dynamic line rating systems for wind power integration&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1364032115007960&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;53&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Fernandez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;E.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Albizu&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;I.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Bedialauneta&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;M.T.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mazon&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;A.J.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Leite&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;P.T.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,19]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,1]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;fernandezReviewDynamicLine2016&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDceF4JsJDpZ"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[11]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;TF67pkp6&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[11]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[11]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:968,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/RLNSVWVG&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:968,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;When a wind power system is connected to a network point there is a limit of power generation based on the characteristics of the network and the loads connected to it. Traditionally, transmission line limits are estimated conservatively assuming unfavourable weather conditions (high ambient temperature, full sun and low wind speed). However, the transmission capacity of an overhead line increases when wind speed is high, due to the cooling caused by wind in the distribution lines.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.rser.2015.07.149&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;13640321&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: fernandezReviewDynamicLine2016&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;80–92&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Review of dynamic line rating systems for wind power integration&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1364032115007960&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;53&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Fernandez&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;E.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Albizu&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;I.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Bedialauneta&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;M.T.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mazon&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;A.J.&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Leite&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;P.T.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,19]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2016&quot;,1]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;fernandezReviewDynamicLine2016&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDceF4JsJDpZ"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. This is referred to as Static Line Rating (SLR). By design, SLR underestimates the capacity of a transmission line and, when implemented in practice, leads to an overestimated transmission capacity. In contrast, Dynamic Line Rating (DLR) calculates the line capacity taking into account the prevailing weather conditions. Cold weather and wind cool overheated transmission lines, enabling the thermal rating to be raised. This results in key benefits in cost-efficiency, congestion reduction and better wind power integration </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;XO9cTeq5&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[12]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[12]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:951,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/I3BT7SXX&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:951,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The need for ﬂexibility in power system operation gradually increases regarding more renewable energy integration, load growth, etc., and the system operators already invest in this manner to enhance the power system operation. Besides, the power system has thermally sensitive assets such as lines, transformers, etc. that are normally operated under highly conservative static ratings. There is a growing trend in this regard to use the actual capacity of such assets dynamically under varying operating conditions leading to a dynamic thermal rating concept which is referred as dynamic line rating (DLR) approach speciﬁcally for lines. This study provides a comprehensive overview of existing perspectives on DLR and combination with other ﬂexibility options from an operational point of view. Apart from the existing review studies more focused on implementation category of DLR concept, the concentration on more operational stage from the power system operation point of view leads the diﬀerence of this study compared to the mentioned studies. A categorization of the DLR implementation for either being sole or combined usage as a ﬂexibility option is further realized. Besides, a geographically categorized analysis on existing practical evidence on DLR concept and implementations is also presented in this study.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energies&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.3390/en13246563&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;1996-1073&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;24&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energies&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: erdincComprehensiveOverviewDynamic2020&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;6563&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A comprehensive overview of dynamic line rating combined with other flexibility options from an operational point of view&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/13/24/6563&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;13&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Erdinç&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;F. Gülşen&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Erdinç&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Ozan&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Yumurtacı&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Recep&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Catalão&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;João P. S.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,12,8]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2020&quot;,12,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;erdincComprehensiveOverviewDynamic2020&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDDcAVx0wG6U"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">[12]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;XO9cTeq5&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[12]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[12]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:951,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/I3BT7SXX&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:951,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The need for ﬂexibility in power system operation gradually increases regarding more renewable energy integration, load growth, etc., and the system operators already invest in this manner to enhance the power system operation. Besides, the power system has thermally sensitive assets such as lines, transformers, etc. that are normally operated under highly conservative static ratings. There is a growing trend in this regard to use the actual capacity of such assets dynamically under varying operating conditions leading to a dynamic thermal rating concept which is referred as dynamic line rating (DLR) approach speciﬁcally for lines. This study provides a comprehensive overview of existing perspectives on DLR and combination with other ﬂexibility options from an operational point of view. Apart from the existing review studies more focused on implementation category of DLR concept, the concentration on more operational stage from the power system operation point of view leads the diﬀerence of this study compared to the mentioned studies. A categorization of the DLR implementation for either being sole or combined usage as a ﬂexibility option is further realized. Besides, a geographically categorized analysis on existing practical evidence on DLR concept and implementations is also presented in this study.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energies&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.3390/en13246563&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;1996-1073&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;24&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;Energies&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: erdincComprehensiveOverviewDynamic2020&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;6563&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A comprehensive overview of dynamic line rating combined with other flexibility options from an operational point of view&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/13/24/6563&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;13&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Erdinç&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;F. Gülşen&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Erdinç&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Ozan&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Yumurtacı&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Recep&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Catalão&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;João P. S.&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,12,8]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2020&quot;,12,12]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;erdincComprehensiveOverviewDynamic2020&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDDcAVx0wG6U"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. So far, the literature provides case studies considering DLR in small scale Energy System Optimization Models (ESOMs) </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;IKXAL3gt&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[13]\\uc0\\u8211{}[15]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[13]–[15]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:969,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/CXVANRKJ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:969,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The integration of large-scale stochastic renewable energy, the aging of transmission facilities, and the growth of load demand all contribute to the increasing congestion levels of transmission systems. Such factors pose considerable stress on the economical and secure operation of power systems and the accommodation of large-scale renewable energies. However, under the smart grid circumstance, some cutting-edge transmission technologies can bring potential cost-effective solutions to leverage the potential capacity of existing transmission infrastructures. Such technologies can help the utilities to deal with the rapid change of operating conditions of the power system in a more flexible manner. For example, the network topology optimization (NTO) technology can change the transmission topology based on the operating conditions, which increases the flexibility of the transmission system. Dynamic thermal rating (DTR) can evaluate the maximum transmission capacity of transmission lines dynamically according to the weather condition parameters around the conductor. These two cost-effective technologies are promising in improving the congestion mitigation performance and can contribute to the efficient utilization of transmission network-so they will bring potential economic and reliability benefits. This paper incorporates NTO and DTR in the network-constrained unit commitment (NCUC) framework to study their synergistic effect on the power system day-ahead schedule. Case studies are performed on a modified RTS-79 system. The numerical results verify that the coordination of NTO and DTR may help decrease the generation cost and wind power curtailment.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Access&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2904877&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2169-3536&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: liDayAheadSchedulingPower2019&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;35287–35301&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Day-ahead scheduling of power system incorporating network topology optimization and dynamic thermal rating&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;7&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Li&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Yanlin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hu&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Bo&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xie&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Kaigui&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Wang&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Lingfeng&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xiang&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Yingmeng&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xiao&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Ruosong&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kong&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Dezhuang&quot;}],&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;liDayAheadSchedulingPower2019&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:973,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/JU77SLL4&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:973,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;This paper addresses the optimal operation and scheduling of reconfigurable microgrids incorporating the dynamic line rating limitations during the islanded and gridconnected mode operations. The incorporation of the dynamic line rating of overhead feeders can potentially improve the system security when providing economical and technical benefits for the microgrid. The proposed framework takes into account a realistic formulation to minimize the total microgrid costs in both grid-connected and multi-period islanded modes. Also, a stochastic framework based on unscented transform (UT) is proposed to model the uncertainties associated with renewable energy sources (RESs) output power, market energy price, load demand as well as the weather uncertain parameters such as solar radiation, wind speed, and ambient temperature. Due to the high nonlinearity and complexity of the proposed problem, an efficient optimization algorithm based on collective decision optimization algorithm (CDOA) is proposed. A new two-stage modification method is also developed to improve the algorithm search ability and avoid premature convergence. The proposed problem is examined on the IEEE 32-bus microgrid test system. The simulation results show the effectiveness of the proposed model and verify its economic and reliability merits.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Transactions on Industrial Electronics&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1109/TIE.2018.2827978&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0278-0046, 1557-9948&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;2&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;IEEE Trans. Ind. Electron.&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: dabbaghjamaneshEffectiveSchedulingReconfigurable2019&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;1552–1564&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Effective scheduling of reconfigurable microgrids with dynamic thermal line rating&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8340159/&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;66&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Dabbaghjamanesh&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Morteza&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kavousi-Fard&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Abdollah&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mehraeen&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Shahab&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,2]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;dabbaghjamaneshEffectiveSchedulingReconfigurable2019&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:899,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/4MNLPLZT&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:899,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Electric Power Systems Research&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;publisher: Elsevier&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;194–208&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Google Scholar&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Day-ahead dispatch optimization with dynamic thermal rating of transformers and overhead lines&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;171&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Viafora&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Nicola&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Morozovska&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Kateryna&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kazmi&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Syed Hamza Hasan&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Laneryd&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tor&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hilber&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Patrik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Holbøll&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Joachim&quot;}],&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;viaforaDayaheadDispatchOptimization2019&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDQyifI5fJ9f"/><text:span text:style-name="T29">[13]</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T28">–[15]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;IKXAL3gt&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[13]\\uc0\\u8211{}[15]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[13]–[15]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:969,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/CXVANRKJ&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:969,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;The integration of large-scale stochastic renewable energy, the aging of transmission facilities, and the growth of load demand all contribute to the increasing congestion levels of transmission systems. Such factors pose considerable stress on the economical and secure operation of power systems and the accommodation of large-scale renewable energies. However, under the smart grid circumstance, some cutting-edge transmission technologies can bring potential cost-effective solutions to leverage the potential capacity of existing transmission infrastructures. Such technologies can help the utilities to deal with the rapid change of operating conditions of the power system in a more flexible manner. For example, the network topology optimization (NTO) technology can change the transmission topology based on the operating conditions, which increases the flexibility of the transmission system. Dynamic thermal rating (DTR) can evaluate the maximum transmission capacity of transmission lines dynamically according to the weather condition parameters around the conductor. These two cost-effective technologies are promising in improving the congestion mitigation performance and can contribute to the efficient utilization of transmission network-so they will bring potential economic and reliability benefits. This paper incorporates NTO and DTR in the network-constrained unit commitment (NCUC) framework to study their synergistic effect on the power system day-ahead schedule. Case studies are performed on a modified RTS-79 system. The numerical results verify that the coordination of NTO and DTR may help decrease the generation cost and wind power curtailment.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Access&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2904877&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2169-3536&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: liDayAheadSchedulingPower2019&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;35287–35301&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Day-ahead scheduling of power system incorporating network topology optimization and dynamic thermal rating&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;7&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Li&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Yanlin&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hu&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Bo&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xie&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Kaigui&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Wang&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Lingfeng&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xiang&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Yingmeng&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Xiao&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Ruosong&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kong&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Dezhuang&quot;}],&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;liDayAheadSchedulingPower2019&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:973,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/JU77SLL4&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:973,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;This paper addresses the optimal operation and scheduling of reconfigurable microgrids incorporating the dynamic line rating limitations during the islanded and gridconnected mode operations. The incorporation of the dynamic line rating of overhead feeders can potentially improve the system security when providing economical and technical benefits for the microgrid. The proposed framework takes into account a realistic formulation to minimize the total microgrid costs in both grid-connected and multi-period islanded modes. Also, a stochastic framework based on unscented transform (UT) is proposed to model the uncertainties associated with renewable energy sources (RESs) output power, market energy price, load demand as well as the weather uncertain parameters such as solar radiation, wind speed, and ambient temperature. Due to the high nonlinearity and complexity of the proposed problem, an efficient optimization algorithm based on collective decision optimization algorithm (CDOA) is proposed. A new two-stage modification method is also developed to improve the algorithm search ability and avoid premature convergence. The proposed problem is examined on the IEEE 32-bus microgrid test system. The simulation results show the effectiveness of the proposed model and verify its economic and reliability merits.&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Transactions on Industrial Electronics&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1109/TIE.2018.2827978&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;0278-0046, 1557-9948&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;2&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;IEEE Trans. Ind. Electron.&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;english&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;Citation Key: dabbaghjamaneshEffectiveSchedulingReconfigurable2019&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;1552–1564&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Effective scheduling of reconfigurable microgrids with dynamic thermal line rating&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8340159/&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;66&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Dabbaghjamanesh&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Morteza&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kavousi-Fard&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Abdollah&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Mehraeen&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Shahab&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2022&quot;,1,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,2]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;dabbaghjamaneshEffectiveSchedulingReconfigurable2019&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:899,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/4MNLPLZT&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:899,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Electric Power Systems Research&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;publisher: Elsevier&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;194–208&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Google Scholar&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Day-ahead dispatch optimization with dynamic thermal rating of transformers and overhead lines&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;171&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Viafora&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Nicola&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Morozovska&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Kateryna&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Kazmi&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Syed Hamza Hasan&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Laneryd&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tor&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hilber&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Patrik&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Holbøll&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Joachim&quot;}],&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;viaforaDayaheadDispatchOptimization2019&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDQyifI5fJ9f"/><text:span text:style-name="T1">. However, it is lacking of an extensive evaluation and assessment of DLR being applied to a higher scale ESOM considering capacity expansion and a CO₂ target.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">This paper addresses this gap. We model and analyze the impact of DLR on the optimal dispatch and deployment of power generators in the German electricity sector. The underlying model is created from the PyPSA-Eur framework <text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;JJB5RpPX&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[16]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[16]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:122,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/4EPNIZU9&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:122,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy Strategy Reviews&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.esr.2018.08.012&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2211467X&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;tex.copyright: All rights reserved\nCitation Key: horsch_pypsa-eur_2018&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;207-215&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Crossref&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;PyPSA-Eur: An open optimisation model of the European transmission system&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;PyPSA-Eur&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2211467X18300804&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;22&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Schlachtberger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;David&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,4,1]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,11]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;horsch_pypsa-eur_2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDdGODmd4jwF"/>[16]<text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;JJB5RpPX&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[16]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[16]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:122,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/4EPNIZU9&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:122,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Energy Strategy Reviews&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.1016/j.esr.2018.08.012&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2211467X&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;tex.copyright: All rights reserved\nCitation Key: horsch_pypsa-eur_2018&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;207-215&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Crossref&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;PyPSA-Eur: An open optimisation model of the European transmission system&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;PyPSA-Eur&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2211467X18300804&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;22&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Schlachtberger&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;David&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2019&quot;,4,1]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2018&quot;,11]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;horsch_pypsa-eur_2018&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDdGODmd4jwF"/>, and for Germany consists of <text:span text:style-name="T30">XX</text:span> representative nodes and considers a time horizon of one year with an hourly resolution. To model the effect of DLR on the power system, we use the IEEE standard <text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;SIKkXG9H&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[17]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[17]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/M5E2F8X7&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;ISBN: 9780738188874\nDOI: 10.1109/IEEESTD.2013.6692858&quot;,&quot;publisher&quot;:&quot;IEEE&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6692858&quot;,&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,7]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStandardCalculating&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDAcngDAaU3I"/>[17]<text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;SIKkXG9H&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[17]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[17]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/M5E2F8X7&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;ISBN: 9780738188874\nDOI: 10.1109/IEEESTD.2013.6692858&quot;,&quot;publisher&quot;:&quot;IEEE&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6692858&quot;,&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,7]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStandardCalculating&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDAcngDAaU3I"/> with highly resolved historical weather data implemented in the Atlite software <text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;vJvl2zul&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SD9T2RK2&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Open Source Software&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2475-9066&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;62&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;JOSS&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;3294&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;atlite: A Lightweight Python Package for Calculating Renewable Power Potentials and Time Series&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;atlite&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://joss.theoj.org/papers/10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hampp&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Johannes&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neumann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,6,24]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;hofmannAtliteLightweightPython2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDpCbAIFPD1W"/>[18]<text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;vJvl2zul&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SD9T2RK2&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Open Source Software&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2475-9066&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;62&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;JOSS&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;3294&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;atlite: A Lightweight Python Package for Calculating Renewable Power Potentials and Time Series&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;atlite&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://joss.theoj.org/papers/10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hampp&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Johannes&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neumann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,6,24]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;hofmannAtliteLightweightPython2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDpCbAIFPD1W"/>. The advantages of the DLR are illustrated by <text:soft-page-break/>comparison with an SLR-based system. Therefore, we analyze and compare curtailment, grid congestion as well as the optimal generator deployment.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P4"/>
    <text:list xml:id="list134534263190757" text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P17">Methodology</text:p>
      <text:list text:style-name="WWNum27">
       <text:list-item>
        <text:h text:style-name="P22" text:outline-level="2"><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T32">Dynamic Line Rating</text:span></text:h>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T12">For simulat</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">ing</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12"> the effect of DLR, we follow the IEEE standard documented in </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;bzHZIyKj&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[17], [19]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[17], [19]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/M5E2F8X7&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;ISBN: 9780738188874\nDOI: 10.1109/IEEESTD.2013.6692858&quot;,&quot;publisher&quot;:&quot;IEEE&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6692858&quot;,&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,7]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStandardCalculating&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:895,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/5YB83TRD&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:895,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Abstract: A method of calculating the current-temperature relationship of bare overhead lines, given the weather conditions, is presented. Along with a mathematical method, sources of the values to be used in the calculation are indicated. This standard does not undertake to list actual temperature-ampacity relationships for a large number of conductors nor does it recommend appropriately conservative weather conditions for the rating of overhead power lines. It does, however, provide a standard method for doing such calculations for both constant and variable conductor current and weather conditions.&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;72&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Zotero&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Std 738™-2012 (Revision of IEEE Std 738-2006/Incorporates IEEE Std 738-2012/Cor 1-2013), IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStd738&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND78tAaGRUxI"/><text:span text:style-name="T12">[17], [19]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;bzHZIyKj&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[17], [19]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[17], [19]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/M5E2F8X7&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:888,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;report&quot;,&quot;note&quot;:&quot;ISBN: 9780738188874\nDOI: 10.1109/IEEESTD.2013.6692858&quot;,&quot;publisher&quot;:&quot;IEEE&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6692858&quot;,&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,7]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStandardCalculating&quot;}},{&quot;id&quot;:895,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/5YB83TRD&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:895,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;abstract&quot;:&quot;Abstract: A method of calculating the current-temperature relationship of bare overhead lines, given the weather conditions, is presented. Along with a mathematical method, sources of the values to be used in the calculation are indicated. This standard does not undertake to list actual temperature-ampacity relationships for a large number of conductors nor does it recommend appropriately conservative weather conditions for the rating of overhead power lines. It does, however, provide a standard method for doing such calculations for both constant and variable conductor current and weather conditions.&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;72&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;Zotero&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IEEE Std 738™-2012 (Revision of IEEE Std 738-2006/Incorporates IEEE Std 738-2012/Cor 1-2013), IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors&quot;,&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;IEEEStd738&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RND78tAaGRUxI"/><text:span text:style-name="T12">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">The methodology was integrated into the Atlite software </text:span><text:reference-mark-start text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;pzzErwAe&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SD9T2RK2&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Open Source Software&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2475-9066&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;62&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;JOSS&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;3294&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;atlite: A Lightweight Python Package for Calculating Renewable Power Potentials and Time Series&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;atlite&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://joss.theoj.org/papers/10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hampp&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Johannes&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neumann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,6,24]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;hofmannAtliteLightweightPython2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDR2WcxBH9zZ"/><text:span text:style-name="T13">[18]</text:span><text:reference-mark-end text:name="ZOTERO_ITEM CSL_CITATION {&quot;citationID&quot;:&quot;pzzErwAe&quot;,&quot;properties&quot;:{&quot;formattedCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;plainCitation&quot;:&quot;[18]&quot;,&quot;noteIndex&quot;:0},&quot;citationItems&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;uris&quot;:[&quot;http://zotero.org/users/7849237/items/SD9T2RK2&quot;],&quot;itemData&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:909,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;article-journal&quot;,&quot;container-title&quot;:&quot;Journal of Open Source Software&quot;,&quot;DOI&quot;:&quot;10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;ISSN&quot;:&quot;2475-9066&quot;,&quot;issue&quot;:&quot;62&quot;,&quot;journalAbbreviation&quot;:&quot;JOSS&quot;,&quot;page&quot;:&quot;3294&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;DOI.org (Crossref)&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;atlite: A Lightweight Python Package for Calculating Renewable Power Potentials and Time Series&quot;,&quot;title-short&quot;:&quot;atlite&quot;,&quot;URL&quot;:&quot;https://joss.theoj.org/papers/10.21105/joss.03294&quot;,&quot;volume&quot;:&quot;6&quot;,&quot;author&quot;:[{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hofmann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hampp&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Johannes&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Neumann&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Fabian&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Brown&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Tom&quot;},{&quot;family&quot;:&quot;Hörsch&quot;,&quot;given&quot;:&quot;Jonas&quot;}],&quot;accessed&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,10,20]]},&quot;issued&quot;:{&quot;date-parts&quot;:[[&quot;2021&quot;,6,24]]},&quot;citation-key&quot;:&quot;hofmannAtliteLightweightPython2021&quot;}}],&quot;schema&quot;:&quot;https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json&quot;} RNDR2WcxBH9zZ"/>, <text:span text:style-name="T39">a Python package used for converting weather data into time-series and energy potential </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T40">for</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T39"> renewable power </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T40">systems</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T39">. </text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P34"><text:span text:style-name="T39">The </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">key concept of DLR is based on a dynamic estimation of </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17">the maximally allowed electrical current </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17"><draw:g text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:y="-0.24cm" draw:z-index="5" draw:style-name="gr1">
       <svg:title>TexMaths</svg:title>
