         </draw:image>
        </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure0" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">1</text:sequence>: Power Rating for different prevailing weather conditions</text:p>
      </draw:text-box>
     </draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P30"/>
    <text:list xml:id="list134534200460178" text:continue-list="list134534263190757" text:style-name="WWNum28">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:list>
       <text:list-item>
        <text:h text:style-name="P23" text:outline-level="2"><text:soft-page-break/>Power System Model<text:span text:style-name="T38">ling</text:span></text:h>
       </text:list-item>
      </text:list>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P40"><text:span text:style-name="T22">Software, Topology data, Power Plant data, approximations, optimization</text:span></text:p>
    <text:list xml:id="list134533283753948" text:continue-list="list134534200460178" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P21">Use <text:s/>Cases</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:h text:style-name="P26" text:outline-level="2"><text:span text:style-name="T31">Effect on </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T33">Optimal O</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T31">perat</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T33">ion of the Existing </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T31">Power </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T33">System</text:span></text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="P38">Germany 2019</text:p>
    <text:h text:style-name="P25" text:outline-level="2">Effect on Optimal Operation and Investment</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="P38">Germany 2030</text:p>
    <text:list xml:id="list134533619237674" text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P18">Results</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:h text:style-name="P27" text:outline-level="2" text:is-list-header="true"/>
    <text:list xml:id="list134534888712631" text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P20">Limitations</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:list xml:id="list33180099" text:style-name="L1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P39">Neglecting Imports and exports</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P39">Some cross-border lines are missing which leads to overestimated curtailment of wind power (in particular Diele &amp; Dörpen West bus have to curtail due to missing link to Netherlands)</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:list xml:id="list134534304677215" text:continue-list="list134534888712631" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P18">Discussion</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P7"/>
    <text:p text:style-name="P12"><text:span text:style-name="T3">Given an adequate size ratio, wind farms together with hydrogen stores can provide a base load preventing high pressure on the transmission system. However, the economic feasibility for a large scale implementation </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">of hydrogen stores </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">is not yet </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">fully </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">given, mainly due to relatively low round-trip efficiencies and </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">(yet) </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">high prices of conversion and storage components. </text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P12"><text:span text:style-name="T5">By converting renewable energy to Methan, Hydrogen </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">of synthetic fuels</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">, the energy can be transported through the existing or new built transport system, as </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">for example </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">planned by the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">European </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">Hydrogen Backbone </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">initiative </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">(Citation). Again, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">this requires high investments as well as a long planning phase, thus cannot be considered as a short term measure. </text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P7"/>
    <text:list xml:id="list134533652243409" text:continue-numbering="true" text:style-name="WWNum4">
     <text:list-header>
      <text:p text:style-name="P19"/>
     </text:list-header>
    </text:list>
    <text:h text:style-name="P5" text:outline-level="5">References</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[1]<text:tab/>O. Kühne and F. Weber, “Conflicts and negotiation processes in the course of power grid extension in Germany,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Landsc. Res.</text:span>, vol. 43, no. 4, pp. 529–541, May 2018, doi: 10.1080/01426397.2017.1300639.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[2]<text:tab/>Federal Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate Action, “Germany’s current climate action status,” Jan. 2022. Accessed: Jan. 28, 2022. [Online]. Available: https://www.bmwi.de/Redaktion/EN/Downloads/E/germany-s-current-climate-action-status.html</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[3]<text:tab/>P. D. B. Burger, “Energy-Charts.” https://energy-charts.info/?l=de&amp;c=DE (accessed Jan. 28, 2022).</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[4]<text:tab/>M. Neukirch, “Protests against German electricity grid extension as a new social movement? A journey into the areas of conflict,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Energy Sustain. Soc.</text:span>, vol. 6, no. 1, p. 4, Dec. 2016, doi: 10.1186/s13705-016-0069-9.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[5]<text:tab/>E. Fernandez, I. Albizu, M. T. Bedialauneta, A. J. Mazon, and P. T. Leite, “Review of dynamic line rating systems for wind power integration,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Renew. Sustain. Energy Rev.</text:span>, vol. 53, pp. 80–92, Jan. 2016, doi: 10.1016/j.rser.2015.07.149.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[6]<text:tab/>F. G. Erdinç, O. Erdinç, R. Yumurtacı, and J. P. S. Catalão, “A comprehensive overview of dynamic line rating combined with other flexibility options from an operational point of view,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Energies</text:span>, vol. 13, no. 24, p. 6563, Dec. 2020, doi: 10.3390/en13246563.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[7]<text:tab/>Y. Li <text:span text:style-name="T26">et al.</text:span>, “Day-ahead scheduling of power system incorporating network topology optimization and dynamic thermal rating,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">IEEE Access</text:span>, vol. 7, pp. 35287–35301, 2019, doi: 10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2904877.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[8]<text:tab/>M. Dabbaghjamanesh, A. Kavousi-Fard, and S. Mehraeen, “Effective scheduling of reconfigurable microgrids with dynamic thermal line rating,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">IEEE Trans. Ind. Electron.</text:span>, vol. 66, no. 2, pp. 1552–1564, Feb. 2019, doi: 10.1109/TIE.2018.2827978.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[9]<text:tab/>N. Viafora, K. Morozovska, S. H. H. Kazmi, T. Laneryd, P. Hilber, and J. Holbøll, “Day-ahead dispatch optimization with dynamic thermal rating of transformers and overhead lines,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Electr. Power Syst. Res.</text:span>, vol. 171, pp. 194–208, 2019.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[10]<text:tab/>J. Hörsch, F. Hofmann, D. Schlachtberger, and T. Brown, “PyPSA-Eur: An open optimisation model of the European transmission system,” <text:span text:style-name="T26">Energy Strategy Rev.</text:span>, vol. 22, pp. 207–215, Nov. 2018, doi: 10.1016/j.esr.2018.08.012.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10">[11]<text:tab/>“IEEE Standard for Calculating the Current-Temperature Relationship of Bare Overhead Conductors,” IEEE. doi: 10.1109/IEEESTD.2013.6692858.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:span text:style-name="T1">[12]<text:tab/>F. Hofmann, J. Hampp, F. Neumann, T. Brown, and J. Hörsch, “atlite: A Lightweight Python Package for Calculating Renewable Power Potentials and Time Series,” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">J. Open Source Softw.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">, vol. 6, no. 62, p. 3294, Jun. 2021, doi: 10.21105/joss.03294.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="P11"/>
   </text:section>
